Good. Only package.json "main" needs updating.

Now design the modules.

### electron/ipc-channels.ts
Channel constants + payload types. Types reference PomodoroState, PomodoroSettings from ../app/lib/pomodoro/types.js.

```ts
export const IPC_CHANNELS = {
  getPomodoroState: "pomodoro:get-state",
  startPomodoro: "pomodoro:start",
  stopPomodoro: "pomodoro:stop",
  skipPomodoro: "pomodoro:skip",
  updatePomodoroSettings: "pomodoro:update-settings",
  reportSelectedActivity: "desktop:report-selected-activity",
  setReminderEnabled: "desktop:set-reminder-enabled",
  pomodoroStateChanged: "pomodoro:state-changed",
  reminderChanged: "desktop:reminder-changed",
} as const;

export type SelectedActivityRef = { categoryId: string; title: string | null; reportedAt: number };
export type PomodoroSnapshot = { state: PomodoroState; selectedActivity: SelectedActivityRef | null; reminderEnabled: boolean };
```

SelectedActivityRef is a data-model entity — maybe define it in desktop-state.ts and re-export in ipc-channels? contracts say shared payload types in ipc-channels.ts. Define both here; desktop-state imports from ipc-channels.

### electron/desktop-state.ts
Functions:
- `parseDesktopRuntimeState(raw: unknown): DesktopRuntimeState` — pure, testable. version must be 1; unknown version → defaults. run via parsePomodoroRun (but parsePomodoroRun returns full PomodoroState including settings; we need run snapshot + settings separately. Hmm: parsePomodoroRun(raw, settings) returns PomodoroState. We can store `run` as the snapshot fields, parse via parsePomodoroRun into a state using parsed settings, then pick the run fields back out. That validates per existing helper. Corrupt run → fallback idle state.
- `loadDesktopState(filePath: string): Promise<DesktopRuntimeState>` (read file, JSON.parse, parse) — real fs, no mocks; tests use temp files.
- `saveDesktopState(filePath, state)` debounced: create a `DesktopStateStore` class with `persist()` debounced to ≤1Hz (e.g. trailing debounce of 1000ms? "debounced writes ≤1 Hz for run snapshots" — meaning at most one write per second). Implement DebouncedPersister class with `schedule(state)` and `flush()`; testable by injecting... tests can use real timers with short interval injection (intervalMs param, default 1000).

DesktopRuntimeState = { version: 1, run: PomodoroRunSnapshot-ish (we can persist the full run fields), settings, selectedActivity, reminderEnabled }.

For run parsing: parsePomodoroRun expects `remainingSeconds` positive int etc. Fine.

save: writes { version: 1, run: {...}, settings, selectedActivity, reminderEnabled }.

Write atomically: write temp then rename? Keep simple: writeFile directly (data-model doesn't demand atomic). I'll do direct write with mkdir parents.

### electron/pomodoro-host.ts
Main-process host. Electron-bound but keep core pure-ish. Design:

```ts
export type PomodoroHostOptions = {
  store: DesktopStateStore;
  tracker: TrackerClient; // thin fetch wrapper, injectable for tests
  broadcast: (snapshot: PomodoroSnapshot) => void;
  now?: () => number;
};
```

TrackerClient interface (D10: fetch stubbed at HTTP boundary):
```ts
export type TrackerClient = {
  getActiveTimer(): Promise<{ id: string; title: string | null; categoryName: string | null } | null>;
  startTimer(input: { categoryId: string; title: string | null }): Promise<string | null>; // returns draft id
  stopTimer(sessionId: string): Promise<boolean>;
};
```
Implement `createHttpTrackerClient(origin: string, fetcher = fetch)` in pomodoro-host or separate; uses GET /api/tracker, POST /api/tracker.

Host class:
```ts
export class PomodoroHost {
  private state: PomodoroState;
  private selectedActivity: SelectedActivityRef | null;
  private reminderEnabled: boolean;
  private boundDraftId: string | null;
  private activityName: { title: string | null; categoryName: string | null } | null; // from polling
  private lastBroadcastAt = 0;

  getState(): PomodoroSnapshot
  async start(): Promise<PomodoroState>
  async stop(): Promise<PomodoroState>
  async skip(): Promise<PomodoroState>
  async updateSettings(partial): Promise<PomodoroState>
  reportSelectedActivity(ref): void
  setReminderEnabled(enabled): boolean
  async tick(): Promise<void>  // 1Hz; advances engine, polls tracker, broadcasts
  async onWindowLoaded(): // immediate broadcast
}
```

Start semantics (D3):
1. const wasIdle = state.phase === "idle" && !state.isRunning? Actually "If idle-start and no tracker draft is active and a selected activity exists, also starts the tracker". Engine start on idle → focus. Resume on non-idle non-running.
2. state = start(state, now).
3. If was idle-start (phase was idle): if no active draft (from last poll: activeTimer === null) and selectedActivity?.categoryId: POST start → record boundDraftId; on 404/409/error → boundDraftId = null (countdown-only).
4. reminder cycle reset hook: host emits onStart callback? T027: "reset on every start action" — host calls `this.onPomodoroStarted?.()`. Better: host accepts `onFirstTick`... Let me wire reminder cycle in main.ts with host callbacks. Provide host.events: { onPomodoroStarted?: () => void; onPomodoroStopped?: () => void }. Actually reminder reset on start action and re-arm when engine transitions to idle. The reminder cycle itself can decide on each of its ticks using shouldRemind; but FR-011 "reset() so starting any pomodoro re-arms". T027 says host/main: "reset on every start action, re-arm when the engine transitions to idle". Simplest: reminderCycle.reset() whenever state changes (any broadcast)? No — reset only on start and on transition to idle (stop or phase completion to idle... engine never goes to idle except stop). Re-arm when the engine transitions to idle = after stop(). Also on hydrate at launch start cycle.

I'll give PomodoroHost a callback `onRunningChange?: (isRunning: boolean) => void` — main wires it to reminderCycle.reset(). Called when isRunning flips from false→true (start) or true→false (stop/phase complete). Hmm "re-arm when the engine transitions to idle" — phase complete transitions to a rest phase (not idle) with isRunning=false — reminders resume counting? FR-011: reset so next reminder lands 5 idle minutes after idle onset. Idle onset = when isRunning becomes false. So reset on any isRunning falling edge. That's fine.

Broadcast: `broadcast(snapshot)` — gating ≤1 Hz is in host: action-originated changes broadcast immediately (lastBroadcastAt = now); tick broadcasts at most 1 Hz. Actually contracts say "at most 1 Hz while running; immediate on any action-originated change; also sent once after a window finishes loading". Implement: broadcast(snapshot, { force }): if force or now - lastBroadcastAt >= 950ms → send. On tick, force=false.

Tick: state = tick(state, now).state; poll tracker (every tick? "refreshed on state changes/ticks" — poll every tick = 1 Hz HTTP polling... that's heavy but per task T016 "refreshed on state changes/ticks with graceful failure". Hmm, polling GET /api/tracker every second against local server — acceptable per contract. But maybe poll only while running and on actions. I'll poll: on action-originated changes, on each running tick, and on window load. Actually to limit load: poll every tick (1Hz) only when running; plus on actions.

Wait — but activity name comes from the tracker draft; when not running, tray shows no name anyway (idle title empty). So poll only when running. Also update activityName used for label.

Persistence: on every state change, store.schedule(...) debounced ≤1 Hz.

`hydrate()` at construction: load from store, tick-reconcile (hydratePomodoroState), also reconcile against wall clock for expired phaseEndsAtMs.

Menu actions from tray go through the same start/stop — same methods, so no separate code path.

### electron/tray-label.ts
Pure:
```ts
export type MenuBarDisplayState = { mode: "idle" | "running"; title: string; activityName: string };
export function resolveActivityName(input: { draftTitle: string | null | undefined; categoryName: string | null | undefined } | null): string
export function truncateActivityName(name: string, max = 24): string
export function computeMenuBarDisplayState(state: PomodoroState, activityName: string): MenuBarDisplayState
```
title: running → `${formatPomodoroCountdown(remainingSeconds)} ${activityName}`.

Also menu model builder? T015 "rebuild the menu from the current MenuBarDisplayState". Add:
```ts
export type MenuBarMenuModel = { canStart: boolean; showResume: boolean; canStop: boolean; reminderEnabled: boolean };
export function computeMenuModel(state, reminderEnabled): MenuBarMenuModel
```
Start disabled while running; Resume shown instead of Start when non-idle non-running; Stop disabled when idle.

Hmm — contracts/tray.md: "Start Pomodoro [disabled while running]" and "Resume [shown instead of Start when phase paused/armed-but-not-running; disabled while running]". So:
- running: Start hidden? "disabled while running" — keep Start visible but disabled? And Resume shown only when non-idle non-running. When running: both disabled. I'll show: if running → show "Start Pomodoro" disabled and "Resume" hidden (since Resume condition non-idle non-running false... wait running with non-idle phase: Resume shown only when non-running. So when running show Start disabled only). When non-idle non-running: show Resume enabled, Start hidden? Or Start disabled + Resume enabled? contracts says Resume "shown instead of Start". So:
  - running → Start (disabled)
  - non-idle non-running → Resume (enabled)
  - idle → Start (enabled)
Stop enabled iff non-idle (or running?). "disabled when idle". Engine stop on idle is no-op; disable when phase === "idle".

### electron/tray.ts
Electron shell:
```ts
